("ol", [
 "**Act I (Iqbal's room).** The scene opens with an Indo-Western fusion piece. Four children, Iqbal (flute), Avinash (tabla), Peter (keyboard) and Shruti (violin), finish rehearsal; posters of Pandit Hariprasad Chaurasia and Shashank Subramanyam hang on the wall. Iqbal reads the newspaper: 'Melody runs in the veins of Miss Shruti Sharma, daughter of the master violinist Nabin Sharma!' Shruti is worried because she has not told her father about the practices and the concert. She explains that her father considers fusion a desecration of the violin and believes only Classical Hindustani music, with ragas and aalaaps, is worthy. Her friends encourage her; she resolves to 'bite the bullet' and tell him at dinner.",
 "**Act II (dinner table).** Shruti is lost in thought. Leela jokes about the dosa. Shruti tells her father about the concert next week. Nabin says 'No is the answer', quoting his rule of one performance in six months ('familiarity breeds contempt'). On hearing that it is an Indo-Western fusion concert, he says he never thought any student, let alone his child, would lose musicality to such an extent. Shruti asks him to attend one practice; he mocks that practice is needed for 'phoo' music and leaves. Leela scolds Shruti for hiding things; Shruti promises not to hide again.",
 "**Act III (Iqbal's house).** Shruti plans to practise one last time and then quit (aside). In the middle of the performance her parents enter and are engrossed; Nabin taps his feet. They clap and praise the soulful performance. Nabin offers the music room for practice. Shruti thanks her parents; Nabin says Leela opened his eyes. Leela tells how Nabin, from a family of traditional vocalists, defied them to play the violin. Nabin admits he underestimated the power of Indian music, says 'each bay, its own wind' and promises to root for the group. Curtain down."]),

